  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya&#x2019;s National Employment Authority, in June 2021, established the first Migrant Resource Center (MRC) in Kenya, playing an active role in the migration landscape. This is the first of six planned MRCs across Kenya by December 2023. The MRC provides two types of services to the migrant workers. Firstly, it provides for safe, orderly and legal migration, which includes providing information on migration procedures to potential migrant workers, facilitating recruitment processes and checks and pre-migration information to support those seeking to migrate for work overseas. Secondly, it also provides on-site return services to the migrants, encompassing handling complaints and supporting dispute resolutions between the workers and the recruitment agencies that facilitated their migration. It further includes referral to support services such as legal, business registration, training and investment opportunities and actively supports in the return and re-integration assistance for migrant workers. Finally, it provides referrals for psychosocial support to the distressed migrant workers and their families, traces and links families to the migrant workers in destination countries working with the support of recruitment agencies and provides feedback from the migrant workers. With support from the Government of Switzerland, ILO supported National Employment Authority to establish a Migrant Resource Centre at Kasarani, Nairobi in June 2021that will offer support to outgoing and returning migrant workers. The support included; technical personnel, capacity development on the provision services to the migrant workers and provision of equipment for the centers. 

From May 2021, ILO strengthened the capacity of Kenya&#x2019;s National Employment Agency to support labour migration centres; both existing and in at least 5 new locations selected from the cities/towns of Mombasa, Eldoret, Kisumu, Kakamega, Nyeri and Garissa. This will enable migrant workers to receive support in the decentralized locations across the country.</narrative>
   </description>
